2026-09-06T21:31Z claude note filed on the author's premise of the same session: he will write a Zotero plugin that serves full text derived from the SDT route and chunks, repackaging PR #6012's chunker. This ticket is the contract zoteus codes against; ticket 0572 is the consumer and is re-cut onto it. Two decisions are the author's and are drafted as an awaiting-ratification entry in DECISIONS.md rather than settled here: whether zoteus consumes the plugin's chunks as final or takes blocks and chunks them itself, and how the token budget crosses the boundary. The `needs-human` label is for those two.

--- body ---
## Context

The author will write a Zotero plugin that serves, over the local API's own
HTTP server, the full text Zotero's structured-document route produces and
chunks made by PR #6012's chunker, repackaged. That replaces the mechanism
ticket 0572 was written against — a zoteus-side reader of the binary
`.zotero-sdt-cache` — with an HTTP read like every other source this design
consumes. This ticket is the contract; 0572 is the consumer.

Three things make the plugin route better than the disk read it replaces, and
they are the reason to spend a contract on it rather than patch 0572 in place.

**It keeps copyleft out of zoteus by construction.** #6012 is Zotero source,
AGPL. 0572 carried an open question — reimplement the chunker from observed
behaviour, under a ruling nobody had made. With the plugin the code runs
inside a separate program the author licenses himself (his ruling,
2026-09-06: the plugin is not bound by zoteus's licence), and zoteus consumes
its HTTP output. Nothing is vendored, so the reimplementation question is
dissolved for this path rather than answered. The licence call is the
author's; what this ticket records is the mechanism that makes it a call he
is free to make.

**It removes the internal-format coupling.** No pack-version gate, no
truncated-pack handling, no schema tracking in zoteus. `SPEC.md` C2's
objection — the pack format is internal and unversioned in any public sense —
stops being ours the moment a versioned contract stands in front of it.

**It reaches text the flat route cannot.** Native SDT ignores
`fulltext.pdfMaxPages`. Measured on doudou 2026-09-06 over the 4 762 PDFs
carrying both a pack and a flat cache: in 486 of the 4 031 current-generation
pairs the flat cache stops at exactly page 100 while the pack continues, a
median 1,79x more text. On documents under the cap the two texts are the same
— median ratio 1,00, both extractor generations — so this, not text quality,
is what the structured route adds.

## Relevant files

- `SPEC.md` §5.2.4 (the extract shim's source selection, re-cut in the same
  change as this ticket), §5.2.2 (the chunk key the response must feed),
  §5.2.7/C3 (the streaming property the paging clause protects), C1, C2.
- `bench/sdt-sitter/` — the existing plugin, which today warms packs and
  deliberately opens no endpoint. Whether the contract lands there or in a
  second plugin is the author's call, not this ticket's.
- `verification/probes/sdt_read.py` — the pack reader. It stops being a port
  target and becomes the oracle the endpoint is checked against.
- `SYNC.md` "Zotero extraction" row — the three version constants
  (`SDT_PACK_VERSION`, `SDT_SCHEMA_VERSION`, `SDT_PROCESSOR_VERSIONS`). The
  watch stays; its consumer moves from our reader to the plugin, and a
  processor bump must still reach our ledger through the contract.

## Actions

1. Draft the endpoint's shape and register it the way Zotero plugins do,
   on `Zotero.Server.Endpoints`, so it is served by the same host and port
   the local API already answers on and needs no second transport.
2. Per attachment key the endpoint serves three things, and a consumer may
   ask for any of them: the blocks (type, flow class, page anchor, text),
   the chunks (#6012's chunker over those blocks, each with its heading path
   and page anchor), and the identities below.
3. **Paged, never whole-document.** C3 fixes peak memory to a section batch,
   and the design's conductor never materializes a document. A request takes
   a cursor and a limit; a response carries the next cursor or says it is the
   last. This is not an optimization to add later: a contract that answers a
   2 913-page volume in one body cannot be made streaming without changing
   every consumer, and the first book is where it would be discovered.
4. **Every response names what produced it**: processor type and version and
   source hash from the pack's own metadata, the pack and schema version, and
   — when chunks are served — the chunker's id, version and geometry. This is
   the clause the ledger stands on. `SPEC.md` §5.2.2 keys a chunk on
   `(text_hash, segmenter id+version, chunker id+geometry)`; with the chunker
   outside our tree, a plugin upgrade that moves a boundary is invisible to
   the key unless the identity crosses the wire. A response whose identity is
   absent or unknown is a fallback trigger, never a default.
5. **Fallback is structural, never a failure.** No plugin, a contract version
   zoteus does not know, an attachment the plugin does not cover, no pack —
   each answers so that zoteus takes the flat path for that attachment and
   records why. That is §5.2.4's existing shape and it carries over unchanged.
6. Version the contract itself, separately from the pack version it hides.
   The pack format may move under the plugin without the contract moving.

## Test

- Red first: an attachment whose pack and flat cache differ in a known way
  (a running head present only in the flat cache) served through the
  endpoint. Assert the response's text is the pack's. Run it before the
  endpoint exists: it must fail there, and a 404 is the failure.
- The endpoint's blocks for `GMAP993G` and `BSMCQ6BM` match
  `verification/probes/sdt_read.py`'s output on the same packs — the reader
  is the oracle. Note that the reader's `block_text` gained list-item
  recursion on 2026-09-06 (PR #399); a comparison against the pre-fix reader
  would agree on everything except lists and would look like a pass.
- Paging: a document of at least 1 000 blocks read through to the last
  cursor reassembles byte-identical to the same document read whole by the
  oracle, and no single response exceeds the declared batch.
- Identity: a response with the chunker identity stripped is refused by the
  consumer and the attachment falls to the flat path.
- Fallback: an attachment with no pack, and a contract version bumped past
  what the consumer knows, each yield the flat path and a recorded reason.

## Verification

- [ ] The endpoint answers on the local API's host and port with no second
      listener and no new port.
- [ ] A 2 913-page volume is read end to end without any single response
      holding more than the declared batch.
- [ ] Every response carries processor, source hash, pack/schema version and,
      when chunks are served, chunker id + version + geometry.
- [ ] With the plugin uninstalled, zoteus indexes exactly as it does today.

## Invariants

- No AGPL code vendored into zoteus. #6012's chunker runs inside the plugin
  and reaches zoteus only as HTTP output.
- The plugin never writes to `storage/`, never changes extraction
  preferences, and never replaces a source attachment — the sitter's existing
  charter, which this must not weaken.
- Absence of the plugin changes nothing about today's path.
- The endpoint is read-only.

## Exit criteria

- [ ] The two decisions in DECISIONS.md 2026-09-06 are ratified: chunk
      consumption, and how the token budget crosses the boundary.
- [ ] The contract is written down here, with its version, before either side
      is coded — it is the interface two programs meet at.
- [ ] The endpoint serves blocks, chunks and identities, paged, with the
      tests above green.
- [ ] Ticket 0572's consumer codes against this contract and its red-first
      test fails against the unfixed tree.
